'' 'wstring( n, ch )' IS THE WIDE HALF of STRING( n, ch ), with its own two entry
'' points -- measured, fb_WstrFill1 and fb_WstrFill2, returning a bare uint16* rather
'' than a descriptor. Only the narrow name was recognised.
''
'' AND 'string(' IS A CALL, NOT A TYPE. Both spellings name a TYPE and an INTRINSIC,
'' and the '(' is what tells them apart -- which is what quirk/len-sizeof.bas says
'' beside the line that needs it. Read as a type, 'len( string( 3, "a" ) )' left
'' '3, "a" )' behind and the whole expression was a parse error.
''
'' The three of them also had no TYPE: measured with '#print typeof', STRING, STRING
'' and 'WSTRING * 1', so 'len( string( 3, "a" ) )' had nothing to measure even once it
'' parsed.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/string-fill.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrFill1
'' GOLDEN: expected/string-fill.out
